WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.639
Hey there, welcome to the Paws at Data Science Hangout. I'm Libby Herron, and this is a recording

00:00:16.639 --> 00:00:22.000
of our weekly community call that happens every Thursday at 12pm US Eastern Time. If

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.240
you are not joining us live, you miss out on the amazing chat that's going on. So find

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.680
the link in the description where you can add our call to your calendar and come hang

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:35.680
out with the most supportive, friendly, and funny data community you'll ever experience.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:41.000
Can't wait to see you there. Today, I'm so excited to introduce our featured leader,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:46.080
Cori Campbell. She's a data scientist in the health data science realm. Cori, thank you

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.279
so much for being here. I would love it if you could introduce yourself, tell us a little

00:00:49.279 --> 00:00:52.000
bit about what you do and something that you like to do for fun.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.799
Great, thanks so much Libby and everybody on the team for having me to the hangout.

00:00:56.799 --> 00:01:02.919
I'm super excited to be here. Yeah, my name is Cori. I'm a health data scientist. My background

00:01:02.919 --> 00:01:09.320
is in quantitative epidemiology and health data science. For a large part of my career,

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:15.180
I was in academia and research based in Europe and the UK and I was leveraging electronic

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:20.919
health data and lots of administrative data sources to generate robust evidence with regards

00:01:20.919 --> 00:01:28.680
to health systems, analysis, drug safety, vaccine safety, those types of things. And

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:34.599
I am based now in Montreal in health data science consulting. So still in a very similar

00:01:34.599 --> 00:01:40.400
world kind of through a different lens, using a lot of the same methods and tools that we've

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:48.639
talked about already that we've mentioned. And yeah, I would say for fun in the last

00:01:48.639 --> 00:01:54.199
year, I've been getting into enjoying the snow again, making that part of my, you know,

00:01:54.199 --> 00:01:58.400
self-care routine, getting back into skiing, all of that stuff. I love a good hike any

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:04.400
time of the year, anytime I can get into nature and kind of feel that reset. So this is a

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.639
beautiful place to be doing it. I'm super lucky.

00:02:06.639 --> 00:02:11.279
Oh, it is extra beautiful. I have only seen pictures, but I'm very jealous. Someday I

00:02:11.279 --> 00:02:17.520
hope to go. Well, I heard tons of topics in there. I put some of them in the chat. So

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.800
these are things that we can think about asking Corey about, including a PhD journey,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:29.520
EHR and admin data, academia to industry, drug safety, but also open source tooling

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.440
for science and collaboration, obviously. And you are a globetrotter. You've been all

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:42.600
over the world. So probably also collaborating across multiple time zones, multiple countries

00:02:42.759 --> 00:02:48.119
and multiple sites. These are all going to be fantastic things to ask Corey about. I

00:02:48.119 --> 00:02:53.800
would love to start with the PhD question. Did you always know you were going to get

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.800
a PhD? Was that always the plan? And what did you get your PhD in?

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:02.000
Oh, that's so funny. That's such a good question. I was just talking about this recently with

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:10.000
some friends. My PhD was in, I guess, pharmaco epidemiology was really the main subject.

00:03:10.479 --> 00:03:16.479
Wait, that's a long couple of words. Can you explain pharmaco epidemiology?

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.839
It's such a long word. And it's only a word I learned when I started a PhD in it, ironically.

00:03:23.039 --> 00:03:29.679
It's kind of, I guess you could say epidemiology quantitative methods with regards to medicine

00:03:29.679 --> 00:03:36.399
use, medicine coverage, sometimes uncovering those mechanisms that are underpinning modes

00:03:36.399 --> 00:03:45.520
of action and that kind of thing. So I guess that's kind of a high level how I would describe

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:53.919
it. And I kind of, I guess, not fell into a PhD, but I just had been really enjoying what I was

00:03:53.919 --> 00:03:58.720
studying on my master's. And it kind of just felt like the natural next progression. I did my

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.919
master's in epidemiology in the Department of Population Health at the University of Oxford.

00:04:03.919 --> 00:04:09.119
And then from there, I moved into the Department of Medicine for my PhD, which I did together

00:04:09.119 --> 00:04:16.079
in collaboration and co-sponsored by GSK. So that was awesome because I had the flavor of academic

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:22.480
research and epidemiology and also the pharma side from them. And I feel like I was super lucky to

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:28.559
learn so much on that process and work with great colleagues. But I would say that I was definitely

00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:34.480
guided by the team and my research interests. And I definitely didn't have a PhD on my radar

00:04:35.679 --> 00:04:43.200
from the beginning. I'm a huge proponent of PhDs, no matter what your motivation might be,

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:47.519
but I wasn't necessarily always motivated to do one or thought that I would do one.

00:04:47.519 --> 00:04:51.600
Well, that's the question that I thought was going to come up quickly was, do you still

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:57.440
recommend a PhD in 2026 and the age of AI and everything? But also I wanted to make sure that

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.600
I understood what epidemiology is, especially for anybody who's not in the science world.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:07.119
Epidemiology is the study of health and disease and populations of people, right?

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.200
Yep. Yep, exactly. It doesn't count how I describe it. Yeah.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:17.760
Okay, great. Well, I have a question from Noor that came in on the Slido, and I would love it

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.600
if Noor would unmute and ask that question live. And I see she's on camera too. Hey, Noor.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:31.119
Hi, I'm Noor. I also came from the PhD land. And my question is, what are tips you would give

00:05:31.119 --> 00:05:36.160
for people transitioning from a more academic space or academic leading space

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.959
to an industry space to not only successfully make that transition, but also thrive?

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.920
Oh, that's a great question. And I think it's still one that I'm in the process of answering,

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:54.959
to be honest. I'm super, super green, super new in kind of the industry space. And I'm not sure

00:05:54.959 --> 00:06:05.359
if you made the same transition or if you'd like to. But I think that kind of seeing both sides of

00:06:05.359 --> 00:06:11.279
the fence less as one versus the other and looking more for the spaces where we're overlapping and

00:06:11.279 --> 00:06:15.679
opportunities to collaborate, I think those are a lot more abundant than a lot of people

00:06:15.679 --> 00:06:22.799
may realize. And that a lot of industry folks are super happy to be reached out to cold emailed

00:06:22.799 --> 00:06:26.799
and to look for collaborations in that regard, kind of in the similar style you would do in

00:06:26.799 --> 00:06:33.119
academia. And I think that especially leveraging those opportunities for exposure at conferences

00:06:33.119 --> 00:06:40.559
and those types of things is really invaluable. But that being said, I was doing the bulk of my

00:06:40.559 --> 00:06:48.000
PhD during COVID, so I actually didn't go to any conferences in person during my PhD. So I feel like

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:55.119
that actually was kind of a blessing because I just had to leverage email, LinkedIn, all of these

00:06:55.119 --> 00:07:00.480
other types of avenues for networking. And I feel like especially in this day and age, it's super

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:07.359
easy to make those connections virtually. So I think one of my biggest pieces of advice is never

00:07:07.359 --> 00:07:12.959
be afraid to send the email, send the DM, just send it. You'll always be surprised what comes of that.

00:07:15.359 --> 00:07:21.760
Yeah, I think that that would be my first piece of advice. Does that answer your question?

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:31.200
Feel free to follow up with anything if there's anything more I could add.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:39.040
I think that talking to people is always the answer. It's always my answer. But it's really

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:45.679
nice to know that it feels like people in this space are open to cold DMs and cold emails. Often

00:07:45.679 --> 00:07:49.519
their emails are listed, right? Like you go to the university website, you go to the department

00:07:50.399 --> 00:07:56.480
website, and their emails are there, they're public, and they might take them a few weeks to get back

00:07:56.559 --> 00:08:02.000
to you, but they generally do. That's my experience when I was, you know, looking at all these

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:08.880
things. Also, look for professors of practice who are working both in academia and industry. I find

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:15.600
that they are so easy to talk to, so easy to sort of like approach and say, I just need some advice

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:21.839
or some help. Thank you so much for the question. We have a question from Natasha, which is,

00:08:21.839 --> 00:08:24.640
what is an average day or week look like for you?

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:33.919
Oh, that's another interesting one. I think that the pace of my day has kind of changed

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:40.159
rapidly from academia to industry. I think that might be one of the major shifts that I've

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:48.880
experienced so far. I had to kind of get used to now arriving to the office and being ready for my

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:55.760
whole plan or my whole timetable to kind of shift majorly and for priorities to almost seem like

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:03.440
they have changed overnight. And now I'm focused on project B, whereas I thought I would be coming

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:08.239
in and focusing on project A and specific deliverables. And that's been an excellent

00:09:08.239 --> 00:09:14.880
lesson because I feel like in academia, because the timelines are kind of so much longer, it can

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:20.080
be kind of easy to stay very planned and very regimented. And especially if your work isn't

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:25.760
particularly interdisciplinary or you're not relying on a lot of other people or moving parts to

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:33.280
deliver things, it can be really easy to work kind of according to your own schedule. So the

00:09:34.239 --> 00:09:39.119
really necessary collaboration and reliance and teamwork that we have on each other in industry,

00:09:39.119 --> 00:09:43.599
I feel, has been super rewarding and a new skill that I've learned really quickly.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:50.239
So on any given day, I'll come in and I usually have a couple tasks or deliverables that I know

00:09:50.239 --> 00:09:55.440
need to be delivered within the next few days, kind of trying to plan week by week. But there's

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:00.719
always things that come up, always fun surprises and changing priorities. A colleague might ask

00:10:00.719 --> 00:10:07.599
you for help on something urgent. That's awesome. You know, we do have typical seasonal flows as

00:10:07.599 --> 00:10:15.200
well, similar to all industries. But yeah, it's super dynamic and exciting. I'm someone who likes

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:20.559
to stay busy and feel challenged kind of all the time. So it's definitely been satisfying in that

00:10:20.559 --> 00:10:27.760
way. Thank you. What about the context of tools? Like what tools do you find yourself working in

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:36.159
day to day? Are you in R, Python, SAS, SPSS? And I'm curious what you use for collaboration as well.

00:10:38.159 --> 00:10:44.320
Oh, well, that's another great question. Previously, when I've been doing a lot of

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:49.840
disciplinary work, especially spanning different countries, we've relied really heavily on

00:10:50.479 --> 00:10:57.440
RStudio and GitHub for sharing our workflows. I feel like that's something that I really love

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:02.799
and is still near and dear to my heart. I'd been undertaking federated analysis before in a

00:11:02.799 --> 00:11:09.440
previous role where we would share the workflow through a GitHub repo, and then each data site

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:15.840
would pull that and run it locally. And we might have some little nuances or debugging to undertake

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.479
kind of at each local site. But by and large, the methods and the processing pipeline was the same

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:26.479
across all the sites. And that was so awesome. And it's really where I kind of fell in love with

00:11:26.479 --> 00:11:33.520
open source and transparent pipelines. So I'm still trying to get managed things every

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:42.080
opportunity I get. Still a lot of stuff day to day in R and RStudio. Trying to, yeah, make Positron

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:47.679
my bread and butter IDE right now, which I'm loving. Loving learning how to navigate that.

00:11:47.679 --> 00:11:54.960
That's been awesome. I'm also new to SAS recently in the last year. So learning a new language has

00:11:54.960 --> 00:12:03.919
been awesome. And definitely feeling challenged in that regard. And then in my personal time,

00:12:03.919 --> 00:12:12.719
I try to keep up to date with, you know, novel methods and open source tools and languages and

00:12:12.719 --> 00:12:19.280
stuff. In my spare time, I'm currently trying to, for example, do some sports analytics stuff,

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:25.599
develop some open source stuff there in Python. So it's really a mix. I feel like it's awesome

00:12:25.599 --> 00:12:32.400
to stay current and try to share things as much as you can. But always across, you know,

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:37.440
all of the places I've been working, always sharing code and workflows with colleagues. And

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:39.840
it's something that I love and always want to be doing.

00:12:40.719 --> 00:12:45.200
Do you feel like that's a skill set that you had to learn? That openness to sharing and

00:12:45.919 --> 00:12:50.400
showing people something that feels a little bit vulnerable sometimes, like other people

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:53.679
seeing your code, you can be like, oh, I don't want to look at that. Because I feel like when

00:12:53.679 --> 00:12:59.119
I came out of school, I had been teaching and tutoring so much that I was really used to just

00:12:59.119 --> 00:13:03.440
working out loud, showing everybody everything I was doing. I was used to live coding already,

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:09.119
screen sharing. And I met others who really, really, really struggled with just showing people

00:13:09.119 --> 00:13:14.000
what they were doing. And I feel like it held them back and it helped me. What do you think

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:19.359
about that? What was your journey there? Oh, that's so interesting. It's awesome that you

00:13:19.359 --> 00:13:22.799
were kind of habituated to that right away. I think that's an excellent skill.

00:13:22.799 --> 00:13:27.280
I got lucky. Everybody go tutor. If you're in school right now, go tutor other people. It'll

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:32.479
teach you some skills. Yeah. Live coding, live debugging is, you're right,

00:13:32.479 --> 00:13:38.400
it's intimidating at first. But I actually, I love doing that with my colleagues. Any chance

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:44.799
we get to debug something together? I really love to do it. I think I totally agree that it

00:13:44.799 --> 00:13:50.320
feels really vulnerable, especially if it's kind of a workflow and analysis you've been working

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:55.919
pretty independently on. And then all of a sudden you feel like, oh, I have to open up this little

00:13:55.919 --> 00:14:00.159
world that I've just been programming, this whole system that I've programmed myself in.

00:14:00.159 --> 00:14:08.080
I think especially these days where I've felt the biggest switch is with academic journals.

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:14.159
I think the tide has kind of turned relatively quickly there where you are really often now asked

00:14:14.159 --> 00:14:20.640
to submit your analytical code or a repository alongside your manuscript. And that's been a big

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:27.280
shift. I'm absolutely in support of it. I think it's all for the better. And it's, I think,

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:33.440
changed the way that a lot of academic teams program their analyses from start to finish

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:42.080
and thinking from the beginning, how can we make this accessible and legible to other people,

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:48.000
reviewers, potential collaborators, that kind of thing. And it's always so, it feels so nice

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:52.960
on the flip side when you learn to love that workflow, when someone uses something you've

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:58.640
written before and they tell you how it's impacted their workflow or they give you tips or

00:14:59.039 --> 00:15:04.320
you know, submit a pull request or those kinds of things. It's so much fun and it really feels

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:10.000
like sharing. So you're right, there was kind of a barrier to entry there, but once I came

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:16.479
around to it, I really learned to love it. Wonderful. Well, Isabella put a link in the chat

00:15:16.479 --> 00:15:21.840
as well to something that Cori mentioned earlier, which was Positron. If you are coming from RStudio

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:26.479
and I'm sure Cori came from RStudio, loving RStudio and using it to Positron, I still love

00:15:26.479 --> 00:15:32.239
RStudio. I still use it as well. But if you are coming from RStudio to Positron and Positron looks

00:15:32.239 --> 00:15:38.400
unwieldy or you're worried about it or you have questions about the change in your experience,

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:44.000
Isabella and I did a walkthrough with RLadies Melbourne and they have a video up already of

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:49.840
the recording. So please, please go watch that. Okay. Hubert had a question on Slido and I would

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:54.080
love it if Hubert could ask that live. I'll stick it in the chat as well.

00:15:58.479 --> 00:16:01.039
Sorry, I was slow with my unmuting over here.

00:16:03.119 --> 00:16:06.799
I am so many. I have my two screens and I lost it. There we go.

00:16:10.719 --> 00:16:18.000
Hi, Cori. My question is, what are some of your data challenges? Is it, you know, you've got

00:16:18.559 --> 00:16:25.599
a wide range of collaborators in many, you know, all over the world. Do you have,

00:16:25.599 --> 00:16:31.039
is it lack of kinds of data? Is it things buried in unstructured data? You know,

00:16:31.039 --> 00:16:34.880
I live with a lot of clinical notes and all the good stuff's in clinical notes.

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:42.080
Yeah, stitching different data sources together. So what are some of the data challenges you face

00:16:42.799 --> 00:16:44.080
and how have you approached those?

00:16:46.479 --> 00:16:52.239
Oh, that's really interesting to think about. And it's nice to hear from someone else who

00:16:52.239 --> 00:16:57.760
appreciates the challenge of clinical notes and getting useful information from that. They're so,

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:01.280
they're so rich, but they're a challenge to work with for sure.

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:08.319
I think that in general, across my health data science experience,

00:17:08.319 --> 00:17:17.439
a lot of the big challenges that I've encountered kind of relate to how different health systems

00:17:17.439 --> 00:17:23.199
operate and how that information flows from general practices or doctors or hospitals into

00:17:23.199 --> 00:17:28.560
those records and how different linkages are undertaken, not just across countries, but even

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:33.839
across different regions within a country, this can look so different place to place or hospital

00:17:33.839 --> 00:17:43.040
to hospital and understanding where things are coming from and why has been huge. And I think

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:49.680
that especially for a lot of lab data or things that we would measure for patients or people kind

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:55.439
of on a repeated basis on a regular basis, you know, the tools to undertake those measurements,

00:17:55.439 --> 00:18:02.640
the assays, those labs, the ways that things are measured can be so variable.

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:09.920
So just learning really how we can reliably identify our populations of interest or a study

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:17.839
sample, given all of this nuance across data is massive. In my previous team in the Netherlands,

00:18:17.839 --> 00:18:25.599
we were harmonizing data for up to 10 countries for any given study. And that was so much fun.

00:18:25.599 --> 00:18:33.599
And we had this extra challenge about language and translating things also. And, you know,

00:18:33.599 --> 00:18:38.640
different things can be collected in some countries or some settings, not in others. In

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:43.280
some countries, it comes from a general practitioner or a family doctor in other

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:51.359
countries, things come from the hospital. So I think it really taught me to think high level

00:18:51.359 --> 00:18:55.359
about a health system and how information flows through different health systems.

00:18:56.319 --> 00:19:02.000
The clinical notes piece is another beast that I haven't had to tackle majorly yet. I'm sure it's

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:06.079
coming and I'm excited for it, but I'm always so interested to hear about the methods other

00:19:06.079 --> 00:19:11.680
people are using for that because they seem so diverse and interesting, but it seems challenging

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:20.079
for sure. Well, Hubert knows something about data harmonization and data modeling across many

00:19:20.079 --> 00:19:26.079
different types of data contributors. If anybody's interested, go watch Hubert's episode

00:19:26.079 --> 00:19:30.079
of the Data Science Hangout as well. It's wonderful to be asked a question by somebody

00:19:30.079 --> 00:19:34.959
who has empathy with what you do, isn't it, Cori? I can tell this person knows that this is a

00:19:34.959 --> 00:19:42.000
challenge. Okay. Well, update on the poll that I put, I put the results into the chat. I'm going

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:46.719
to go ahead and stop that one. I asked, do you love being asked for help by coworkers? Because

00:19:46.719 --> 00:19:51.280
Cori mentioned part of her week sometimes is like being asked for help. And she was like,

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:58.319
that's always fun, right? I think so too, but not everybody does. So we had about 17, 18% said, no,

00:19:58.319 --> 00:20:05.040
about 82, 83% said, yes, I love it. Count me in the yes. Nerd snipe me anytime. I have to stop

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:12.560
myself from being nerd sniped because if that could be my whole job, I would love it. I see

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:17.040
some people shaking their heads, yes, in the chat as well. Thank you, Hubert, for that great

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:21.920
question. We had an anonymous question. I'm so curious what you think about this one. It says,

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:27.119
I have been told at work that I need to write more journal articles. I'm good at doing the

00:20:27.119 --> 00:20:33.680
analysis, but I get writer's block when writing anything up. Do you have any tips? Oh my gosh,

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:38.800
I totally empathize with whoever sent this question. And I feel like it's a sentiment

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:45.280
that's shared kind of widely across a lot of research jobs, research settings. I hear a lot

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:54.400
of people say that they're allergic to writing, which I think is not uncommon. It's a great

00:20:54.400 --> 00:21:03.520
question. I feel like on a high level, trying to think about the narrative or the story that your

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:08.719
analysis tells can maybe help you find that motivation for writing sometimes. That's a piece

